 <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 italic">A charger can pass CE, carry an FCC report, and hold a Qi certificate — and still be illegal to sell in Japan. Japan regulates 457 electrical appliance items; 116 of them, including AC adapters and DC power supplies, require the diamond PSE route and a registered-body conformity assessment. The missing piece is usually not the product. It is the Japanese importer's PSE classification, METI notification, and product label.</p><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">That gap is easy to miss. Supplier quotes often compress three different regimes into one sentence: "PSE certified." In practice, a charger sold in Japan may need three things. It may need a safety mark under DENAN, a business filing by the importer, and — for some designs — a radio certification under the Radio Law. Each has a different regulator, a different applicant, and a different document trail.</p>
 </div>
</div>

