<section id="short-answer" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. The short answer: PSE vs METI vs Giteki</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 font-semibold leading-relaxed mb-4">PSE, METI notification, and Giteki are often used as if they were interchangeable. They are not.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl"><table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden"><thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white"><tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Regime</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What it is</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Who regulates it</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What it proves</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What it does not prove</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200"><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm"><strong>PSE</strong></td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Safety mark under the Electrical Appliances and Materials Safety Act (DENAN)</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">METI, with conformity assessment by registered bodies</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The product meets Japan's technical safety requirements for its category</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">That the importer has notified METI, or that a radio module is approved</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm"><strong>METI notification</strong></td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Business filing by the importer ("notifying supplier")</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">METI regional bureau</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The importer has registered the business and accepted DENAN obligations</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">That the product itself has been tested or approved</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm"><strong>Giteki</strong></td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Radio Law technical conformity mark</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">MIC, through registered certification bodies</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The radio equipment meets Japan's Radio Law technical standards</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">That the mains safety requirements are met</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For a mains-powered charger, <strong>PSE almost always applies</strong>. The METI notification is a separate obligation on the importer. Giteki depends on whether the product contains radio equipment and on the wireless power technology.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical rule is simple: <strong>PSE is a product-safety question, METI notification is an importer question, and Giteki is a radio question.</strong> Treat them as three checkboxes, not one.</p>
<div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange rounded-r-xl p-6 mt-6"><p class="text-badge font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Importer Rule</p><blockquote class="text-slate-700 text-base italic leading-relaxed">A PSE certificate for a similar model is not a compliance file for the model you are importing. Match the certificate to the factory, the model, the adapter, and the importer named on the label.</blockquote></div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="denan-scope" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">2. What Japan PSE certification for charger importers covers</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Japan's Electrical Appliances and Materials Safety Act regulates <strong>457 designated items</strong>. METI divides them into two categories. For a DENAN charger, the first question is whether the product is Specified or Non-Specified. The answer determines the mark, the conformity route, and the records the importer must keep.</p>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li><strong>116 Specified Electrical Appliances and Materials</strong> (Category A) — higher-risk products, including AC adapters, DC power supplies, chargers, power cords, plugs, transformers, and certain batteries. These require conformity assessment by a METI-registered body and carry the <strong>diamond PSE mark</strong>.</li><li><strong>341 Non-Specified Electrical Appliances and Materials</strong> (Category B) — lower-risk products. These require self-inspection and record-keeping and carry the <strong>round PSE mark</strong>.</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Act places five obligations on the notifying supplier:</p>
<ol class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5"><li><strong>Notify METI</strong> of the import business.</li><li><strong>Comply with the technical standards</strong>, perform self-inspection, and keep records.</li><li><strong>Obtain conformity assessment</strong> from a registered body for Specified items.</li><li><strong>Affix the PSE mark</strong> only after the required steps are complete.</li><li><strong>Do not sell or exhibit</strong> covered products without the PSE mark.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A common misunderstanding is that METI "certifies" a product. METI does not issue a product certificate. The conformity assessment for a Specified item is performed by a registered conformity assessment body, and the notifying supplier remains responsible for the declaration, labeling, and records.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Three myths are worth correcting up front:</p>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li><strong>"PSE is optional for chargers."</strong> It is not. A mains-connected charger is in scope.</li><li><strong>"A CE or FCC report is enough for Japan."</strong> It is not. Japan applies its own technical standards and its own conformity process.</li><li><strong>"The factory's certificate covers the importer."</strong> It does not. Each Japanese importer must notify METI, put its name on the product, and keep its own records.</li></ul>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="diamond-vs-round" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. Diamond vs round PSE certification: which mark applies to a charger?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The mark type is not a marketing choice. It follows the product category and the conformity route.</p>
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Diamond PSE (Specified)</h3>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li>Requires <strong>conformity assessment by a METI-registered body</strong> and a <strong>factory inspection</strong>.</li><li>The certificate is valid for a fixed period — commonly <strong>3, 5, or 7 years</strong>, depending on the product.</li><li>The <strong>certificate holder must be the actual manufacturer (factory)</strong>. A trading company or design house cannot hold the diamond certificate.</li><li>If the same model is produced in multiple factories, <strong>each factory needs its own certificate</strong>.</li><li>Power cords, attachment plugs, and connectors are certified as separate items; a "cord set" is not one collective category.</li></ul>
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Round PSE (Non-Specified)</h3>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li>Based on <strong>self-declaration and self-inspection</strong> against the technical standards. Testing can be outsourced, but the notifying supplier remains responsible.</li><li>No factory inspection and no fixed certificate validity.</li><li>Inspection records must be kept — typically for <strong>3 years</strong>.</li><li>The importer still must notify METI and label the product.</li></ul>
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Round vs diamond PSE: the charger classification question</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">METI, JET, and TÜV SÜD classification material consistently places <strong>AC adapters, DC power supplies, and chargers</strong> in the Specified category. A <strong>wireless charging pad</strong> sold as a separate mains-powered appliance may be classified as Non-Specified. The classification depends on its construction. If the pad is supplied with an external AC adapter, that adapter is generally a Specified item.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Do not turn that into a universal rule. Product construction, rated input, and whether the adapter is sold separately can change the classification. Confirm the exact category — and whether the product is treated as one appliance or a combination — with a METI-registered conformity assessment body before printing a mark.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl"><table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden"><thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white"><tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Product scenario</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Likely PSE category</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Why</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Importer action</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200"><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">External AC adapter or charger</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm"><strong>Diamond PSE</strong></td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">AC adapters and DC power supplies are Specified items</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Verify the factory-held certificate; notify METI; label the importer name</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">USB charger sold with a separate AC adapter</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Adapter: diamond. Device: confirm</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The adapter and the device may be separate items</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Hold the adapter certificate; confirm the device classification</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Wireless charging pad, mains-powered</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Confirm: pad may be round; adapter is diamond</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Classification depends on construction and power path</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Confirm both classifications with a CAB before printing marks</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Power bank with AC plug or high-energy battery</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Confirm; high energy density may be diamond</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Battery energy density and construction affect the category</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Confirm the battery and product classification</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Charger with Bluetooth or Wi-Fi</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">PSE for the mains part; Giteki for the radio</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The radio module is a separate regime</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Check module certification and host labeling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The table is a planning tool, not a legal ruling. The cost of guessing is a label reprint, a customs hold, or a marketplace delisting — all more expensive than a classification email to a registered body.</p><div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
