<section id="giteki" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. Does your wireless charger need Giteki too?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">PSE and Giteki are separate regimes. PSE is about electrical safety; Giteki is about radio equipment under the Radio Law, administered by MIC and issued through registered certification bodies. TELEC is one such body, not the name of the certification itself.</p>
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">PSE vs Giteki for wireless chargers</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The applicability question depends on the charging technology:</p>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li><strong>Basic Qi induction charging</strong> (around 110–205 kHz) is often treated as outside the Radio Law. That is a common industry position, but it is not a universal rule.</li><li><strong>6.78 MHz or 920 MHz wireless power transfer</strong> systems are more likely to fall within MIC's radio equipment rules.</li><li><strong>Chargers with Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, or other data radios</strong> need the radio module certified; the host device must also follow the labeling rules for certified modules.</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Giteki mark includes the <strong>R symbol and the certification number</strong>. For a host device that contains a certified module, the label may include a statement that the equipment contains radio equipment certified under the Radio Law.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical answer is: <strong>PSE does not cover radio, and a Qi certificate does not cover either regime.</strong> Ask the factory which frequency band the product uses. Ask whether a radio module is present, and which certification number covers that module. Then confirm the scope with a registered certification body.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For the WPC process and how Qi certification differs from PSE and Giteki, see our <a href="/blog/qi-certification-guide/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">Qi certification guide</a>.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="meti-notification" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. METI notification: who files, when, and what it is not</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Japanese importer — the "notifying supplier" — must file an <strong>Electrical Appliances Import Business Notification</strong>. The filing goes to the regional Bureau of Economy, Trade and Industry within <strong>30 days of starting the import business</strong>.</p>
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">METI electrical appliances notification: who files</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Key points:</p>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li>Notification is required <strong>per classification</strong> of electrical appliance. A change in the notifying supplier's name or address, the classification, or the factory requires a notification of change.</li><li><strong>Each importer must notify independently.</strong> One importer's notification does not cover another importer, even for the same factory and model.</li><li>A domestic importer buying from an already-notified overseas business <strong>must itself notify</strong>.</li><li>Only a Japan-resident individual or a Japan-incorporated company can be the notifying supplier. A foreign company without a Japanese office needs a registered representative in Japan.</li><li>Filing is available through <strong>Hoan Net</strong> (online, with gBizID) or on paper to the regional bureau.</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">METI notification is <strong>not</strong> a product certificate, a test report, or a substitute for PSE conformity assessment. It is the importer's registration and acceptance of the DENAN obligations. A product can have a valid PSE certificate and still be non-compliant if the importer has not notified METI or has not put its name on the label.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="specified-importer" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. The December 2025 change: overseas sellers as Specified Importers</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The 2024 amendment to Japan's four product-safety laws took effect on <strong>December 25, 2025</strong>. It changes the route for overseas businesses that sell regulated products directly to Japanese consumers. That includes cross-border e-commerce marketplaces, their own websites, and fulfillment services, where there is no traditional domestic importer.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Under the amended rules, those overseas sellers are defined as <strong>Specified Importers (特定輸入事業者)</strong>. They must:</p>
<ol class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5"><li>Appoint a <strong>Domestic Administrator (国内管理人 / 日代)</strong> with a Japanese address and Japanese-language capability.</li><li>Register with METI as a Specified Importer and obtain a notification document.</li><li>Obtain conformity certification from a registered body.</li><li>Report to METI within 30 days of starting business.</li><li>Keep self-inspection records; the Domestic Administrator must retain them.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Marketplace and fulfillment providers also have obligations to delist non-compliant products. The practical effect is that the "I sell through Amazon Japan, so the platform is the importer" assumption no longer works for every model. The seller may itself be the regulated importer.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">There are now two common routes:</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl"><table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden"><thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white"><tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Route</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Who is the notifying supplier?</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What the overseas brand must do</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200"><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Traditional domestic importer</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The Japanese importer that buys and resells</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Provide the factory certificate, duplicate certificate, test records, and label artwork to the importer</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Cross-border e-commerce (Specified Importer)</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The overseas seller, registered with METI</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Appoint a Domestic Administrator, register, obtain conformity certification, report within 30 days, keep records</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you sell through a Japanese distributor, confirm in writing which party is the notifying supplier and which party's name appears on the product. If you sell direct, plan for the Specified Importer route before your first shipment.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="j62368" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">7. The April 2026 J62368-1 transition</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">METI published <strong>J62368-1(2023)</strong> — harmonized to IEC 62368-1:2018 (3rd edition) — on May 1, 2023, replacing J62368-1(2020). The transition period ends <strong>April 30, 2026</strong>.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The treatment differs by PSE category:</p>
<ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5"><li><strong>Round PSE</strong> certificates must be upgraded to the 2023 standard before the deadline.</li><li><strong>Diamond PSE</strong> certificates generally remain valid until their stated expiry. New applications should use J62368-1(2023).</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Most chargers are diamond PSE, so an existing certificate may not expire on April 30, 2026. That does not mean the date is irrelevant. Check three things:</p>
<ol class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5"><li>Which standard edition the certificate cites.</li><li>The certificate's expiry date.</li><li>Whether the factory plans to renew on the 2023 edition.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask the factory and the registered body for a written answer. Do not rely on a supplier's one-line statement that "the certificate is still valid" without the standard edition and expiry date.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
<section id="importer-checklist" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">8. What Japan PSE certification for charger importers requires</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The importer's checklist is broader than the product certificate. Use this as a pre-shipment review.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl"><table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden"><thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white"><tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">#</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Item</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200"><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">1</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">PSE classification</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Specified (diamond) or Non-Specified (round); confirm with a registered body</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">2</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Adapter classification</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Whether the AC adapter is a separate Specified item</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">3</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Conformity certificate</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Certificate number, CAB, model, factory, standard edition, expiry</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">4</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Factory coverage</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Each manufacturing factory has its own certificate if required</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">5</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Duplicate certificate</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The factory provides a duplicate for the Japanese importer</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">6</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">METI notification</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Filed within 30 days; notification document retained</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">7</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Label</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">PSE mark, notifying supplier name, rated current, CAB name; importer name on the product</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">8</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Records</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Self-inspection/test records retained for the required period</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">9</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Japanese manual and warnings</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Correct language and safety instructions</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">10</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Radio compliance</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Giteki mark/number if the product contains radio equipment</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">11</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Change process</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Supplier, classification, or factory changes trigger a notification of change</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">12</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Accident reporting</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Importer has a process for incidents and recalls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The label is where many otherwise compliant files fail. For a Specified item, the label must identify the notifying supplier and the registered conformity assessment body, in addition to the PSE mark and ratings. If the label shows only the factory name, or the importer name is missing, the file is incomplete.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Read the label as the importer will see it on the product, not only in the manual. Check the PSE mark, the notifying supplier's name, the rated input, and — for a Specified item — the registered conformity assessment body's name. If the product is sold under your brand, confirm how your importer name appears and whether the factory's certificate matches the model on the label. A label that shows only the factory name is not a complete Japanese importer file.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Keep the notification document, certificate copy, test records, and label artwork together. A future model change, factory change, or importer change can trigger a new notification or certificate; a file that is complete today can become incomplete after a production change.</p><div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
